<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The capabilities this app actually claims. There is exactly one, and it is
  here because the button that needs it already exists:
  `Alma/Screens/Settings/SignInScreen.swift` puts a `SignInWithAppleButton` on
  the sign-in screen and hands the credential to
  `AlmaSessionModel.signInWithApple`.

  Without this entitlement the button still draws, still animates, and then
  fails at the moment of tapping with ASAuthorizationError code 1000 ("The
  operation couldn't be completed") — a message that names nothing and sends
  people looking through the networking layer. On a device it fails at install
  time instead, because the provisioning profile does not carry a capability
  the binary asks for. Both failures are the same missing file.

  Two halves, and this is only one of them. The other is in the developer
  portal: the App ID `ai.pazl.alma` must have **Sign In with Apple** enabled
  (Certificates, Identifiers & Profiles → Identifiers → ai.pazl.alma →
  Sign In with Apple → Enable). Xcode's automatic signing will add it to the
  profile it generates, but only once the identifier allows it, and the error
  when it does not is "Provisioning profile doesn't include the
  com.apple.developer.applesignin entitlement".

  `Default` and not `Sign In with Apple at Work & School`: the second is for
  Managed Apple IDs issued by an organisation, which is not who buys this.

  This file sits beside Info.plist rather than inside `Alma/`, for the same
  reason that one does — `Alma/` is a file-system synchronized group, so a
  plist dropped in there is copied into the app bundle as a stray resource.

  There is deliberately no Associated Domains entitlement. Android's manifest
  claims https://alma.pazl.ai/sign-in with autoVerify, but nothing in the iOS
  sources handles an incoming URL or NSUserActivity, so an entitlement here
  would advertise a capability the app does not implement — and Apple checks
  that the apple-app-site-association file exists on the host, which today
  does not resolve at all.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.developer.applesignin</key>
	<array>
		<string>Default</string>
	</array>

	<!--
	  Push Notifications. `development` here, and that is not a placeholder —
	  it is what a locally built binary must carry, and Xcode rewrites it to
	  `production` for anything it archives for distribution. The two are not
	  interchangeable and the failure when they are wrong is silent in one
	  direction: a production token handed to the APNs sandbox host is accepted
	  with a 200 and delivered to nobody. `docs/PUSH.md §1.8`, which also names
	  the trap that catches everybody — a TestFlight build is a *distribution*
	  build, so its tokens are production tokens even though nothing about
	  installing it feels like a release.

	  `PushService.environment` is the other half of this: it reports
	  `sandbox` from a `#if DEBUG` build and `production` otherwise, and sends
	  that alongside the token, so the server never has to guess which of its
	  two APNs hosts a given row belongs to.

	  Two halves, and this is only one of them — same as Sign In with Apple
	  above. The App ID `ai.pazl.alma` must have **Push Notifications** enabled
	  in Certificates, Identifiers & Profiles before automatic signing can put
	  this in a profile, and the error when it has not is "Provisioning profile
	  doesn't include the aps-environment entitlement".

	  **And that is currently blocked.** `STATUS.md §4②` records that the
	  product-id prefix is unchosen — `alma.` versus `ai.pazl.alma.` — and the
	  bundle identifier *is* the `apns-topic` a push is addressed to. Enabling
	  the capability on an App ID that is about to be renamed would have to be
	  undone. The entitlement is here because the code that needs it is here and
	  a simulator build does not check it; the portal half waits for that
	  decision.
	-->
	<key>aps-environment</key>
	<string>development</string>
</dict>
</plist>
